  "prompt": "What does `/[^0-9]/` match, and which test strings contain a match?\n\n```regex\n/[^0-9]/\n```\n\nTest strings:\n\n- `a1` → match\n- `123` → NO match\n- `` → NO match\n- `42a` → match\n- `1.5` → match",
  "instructions": [
    "State which strings match and which do not, and explain why."
  ],
  "constraints": [],
  "examples": [],
  "hints": [
    "^ has a completely different meaning inside [ ].",
    "Is the dot a digit?"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "summary": "The pattern `/[^0-9]/` behaves as shown above.",
    "reasoning": [
      "The leading ^ INSIDE a class negates it: [^0-9] is \"any single character that is not a digit\".",
      "\"a1\" and \"42a\" contain a non-digit; \"1.5\" matches the dot.",
      "\"123\" is all digits, and the empty string has nothing to match — both fail.",
      "Negated classes still match exactly ONE character per repetition; [^0-9]+ would consume \"a\" entirely."
    ],
    "commonMistakes": [
      "Reading ^ at class start as the string-start anchor — meaning flips by position.",
      "Assuming [^0-9] excludes only 0-9 but also excludes punctuation — it includes everything else, dots included."
    ]
  },
  "learningObjectives": [
    "Read negated classes correctly",
    "Distinguish the two meanings of ^"
  ],
